moe_prefill_down_mx.cu     moe_prefill_down.cu on the MX tile (below)

The header and the down projection are the GLM-5.3 versions of this helper
(../glm5_next, same changes): MLX affine codes of 4 or 8 bits with bf16
scales and biases, 64 weights a group, each weight rounded once to bf16 from
float, and each mma.m16n8k16 step added to the running sum with a correctly
rounded float add. The launch geometry and the tensor-core main loop are
unchanged. Added here: 2-bit codes, one 16-byte load a group of 64 decoded
through the group's 4 values (Inkling 2 uses them); 3-bit codes (MLX's
little-endian bit stream), which a thread loads as the 24 bytes of one group
and unpacks to the same bf16 tile;
MLX mxfp4 codes (E2M1, one E8M0 scale per 32 weights, MX template value),
each decoded from the bits of its fp4 value times the power-of-two scale,
which float and bf16 hold exactly. The down projection takes the same
format choice, so it is no longer the GLM-5.3 file unchanged.

The gate/up epilogue is DeepSeek V4.1's: gate and up round to bf16, are
clamped by the swiglu limit, silu(gate) * up is multiplied by the route
weight in float and rounded to bf16, and each group of 32 values takes the
FP8 (E4M3, power-of-two scale) round trip when the down projection quantizes
its input. The helpers of that math come from the family's kernels.py.

The MX tile (msv_expert_tile_mx, sm_120a / sm_121a) keeps msv_expert_tile's
launch geometry, staging and K order and swaps its arithmetic: A rows are
E4M3 codes with one E8M0 scale per 32 (the activation's FP8 round trip kept
as codes), B rows are the mxfp4 codes widened to one byte each, and each k32
step runs mma.sync.kind::mxf8f6f4.block_scale (E4M3 x E2M1, UE8M0 scales)
from zero and is added with a correctly rounded float add. The products are
the bf16 tile's exact products. The gate/up epilogue is unchanged up to the
FP8 round trip, which it writes as codes and scales for the MX down projection.
One MX pass covers MT tiles of 64 routes of an expert (template value MT), so
each staged weight tile serves all of them and the weight slab is read once a
pass instead of once a 64-route tile. Each output keeps its k32 order.

